# Task 01 – Git Exercises

## Overview

For this task, I completed the Git Exercises to strengthen my understanding of Git and version control. The exercises covered important Git concepts such as commits, branches, merging, rebasing, conflict resolution, cherry-picking, resetting, and manipulating commit history.

The goal of this task was not only to complete the levels, but also to understand why each Git command was used and how it affects the repository and commit history.

---

# Exercises

> **Note:** Replace or adjust the commands below if your exact solution for a level was different.

## 1. Commit

### Command Used

```bash
git commit
```

### Explanation

The `git commit` command creates a new commit containing the changes that have been staged. A commit acts like a saved checkpoint in the project's history.

I used this command to create the required commit and move the repository to the expected state.

---

## 2. Branch

### Command Used

```bash
git branch <branch-name>
git checkout <branch-name>
```

Or:

```bash
git checkout -b <branch-name>
```

### Explanation

A branch allows development to happen independently from the main branch.

The `git branch` command creates a new branch, while `git checkout` switches to it. The `-b` option can create and switch to the branch in a single command.

---

## 3. Merge

### Command Used

```bash
git merge <branch-name>
```

### Explanation

The `git merge` command combines the changes from another branch into the currently checked-out branch.

I used it to bring the commits from one branch into another and combine their histories.

---

## 4. Rebase

### Command Used

```bash
git rebase <branch-name>
```

### Explanation

Rebasing moves the commits from the current branch and reapplies them on top of another branch.

Unlike merging, rebasing can create a cleaner and more linear commit history.

---

## 5. Fast-Forward Merge

### Command Used

```bash
git merge <branch-name>
```

### Explanation

When the current branch has no new commits after the point where the target branch was created, Git can perform a fast-forward merge.

Instead of creating a new merge commit, Git simply moves the branch pointer forward.

---

## 6. Merge Conflict

### Commands Used

```bash
git merge <branch-name>
```

After resolving the conflict:

```bash
git add <file-name>
git commit
```

### Explanation

A merge conflict occurs when Git cannot automatically decide which changes should be kept.

I manually edited the conflicting file, selected the correct content, staged the resolved file using `git add`, and created a commit to complete the merge.

---

## 7. Rebase Conflict

### Commands Used

```bash
git rebase <branch-name>
```

After resolving the conflict:

```bash
git add <file-name>
git rebase --continue
```

### Explanation

A conflict can also occur during a rebase.

After manually resolving the conflict, I staged the corrected file using `git add` and used `git rebase --continue` to continue applying the remaining commits.

---

## 8. Cherry-Pick

### Command Used

```bash
git cherry-pick <commit-hash>
```

### Explanation

The `git cherry-pick` command copies a specific commit from another branch and applies it to the current branch.

I used this when only one particular commit was required instead of merging the entire branch.

---

## 9. Reset

### Command Used

```bash
git reset --hard <commit-hash>
```

### Explanation

The `git reset` command moves the current branch to a specified commit.

Using the `--hard` option also updates the working directory and staging area to match that commit.

This was useful when the repository needed to return to a previous state.

---

## 10. Revert

### Command Used

```bash
git revert <commit-hash>
```

### Explanation

`git revert` creates a new commit that reverses the changes made by an earlier commit.

Unlike `git reset`, it does not remove commits from the history, making it useful when the existing history should be preserved.

---

## 11. Amend

### Command Used

```bash
git commit --amend
```

### Explanation

The `--amend` option modifies the most recent commit.

It can be used to change the commit message or include additional changes in the previous commit.

---

## 12. Interactive Rebase

### Command Used

```bash
git rebase -i <commit>
```

### Explanation

Interactive rebase allows commits to be edited, reordered, squashed, or removed.

I used this to manipulate the commit history and arrange the commits into the required order.

---

## 13. Squashing Commits

### Command Used

```bash
git rebase -i HEAD~<number>
```

Then:

```text
pick <commit>
squash <commit>
```

### Explanation

Squashing combines multiple commits into a single commit.

I used interactive rebase and changed the required commits from `pick` to `squash` so that multiple commits could be combined into one.

---

## 14. Reordering Commits

### Command Used

```bash
git rebase -i HEAD~<number>
```

### Explanation

Interactive rebase can also be used to change the order of commits.

I rearranged the commit lines in the interactive rebase editor so that Git replayed them in the required order.

---

## 15. Deleting a Commit

### Command Used

```bash
git rebase -i HEAD~<number>
```

### Explanation

To remove a commit from history, I used interactive rebase and deleted the corresponding commit line or changed its action to `drop`.

Git then recreated the branch history without that commit.

---

## 16. Changing a Commit

### Commands Used

```bash
git rebase -i HEAD~<number>
```

Then:

```text
edit <commit>
```

After making changes:

```bash
git add <file-name>
git commit --amend
git rebase --continue
```

### Explanation

I used interactive rebase to stop at a specific commit and modify it.

After editing the required changes, I staged them, amended the commit, and continued the rebase.

---

## 17. Creating and Switching Branches

### Command Used

```bash
git switch -c <branch-name>
```

Or:

```bash
git checkout -b <branch-name>
```

### Explanation

This command creates a new branch and immediately switches to it.

It is useful when starting a new line of development from the current commit.

---

## 18. Viewing Commit History

### Command Used

```bash
git log
```

### Explanation

The `git log` command displays the commit history of the repository.

I used it to identify commit hashes and understand the structure of the repository before performing operations such as rebasing, resetting, or cherry-picking.

---

## 19. Viewing the Current Repository State

### Command Used

```bash
git status
```

### Explanation

The `git status` command shows the current state of the working directory and staging area.

It helps identify modified, staged, and untracked files and was useful for checking whether the repository was in the correct state before committing or continuing an operation.

